WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
- The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.340 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.730 align:middle line:90%
is available at ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:33.832 align:middle line:90%


00:00:33.832 --> 00:00:35.840 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: OK, welcome back.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.290 align:middle line:90%


00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.500 align:middle line:84%
We just finished our discussion
of predator, prey, and host

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:47.300 align:middle line:84%
parasites illustrating
it with ways

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:53.180 align:middle line:84%
that we can have an impact
on ecological modeling

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.230 align:middle line:90%
in oceans and in public health.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.800 align:middle line:84%
And these kind of
considerations start

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.030 align:middle line:84%
getting us into what are
the global and socioeconomic

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.950 align:middle line:90%
considerations?

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:10.460 align:middle line:84%
What kind of impact do
these kind of models

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.970 align:middle line:90%
have on how we make decisions?

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:23.310 align:middle line:84%
And in this context, I'm glad
to be associated with the Genome

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:23.810 align:middle line:90%
Project.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.360 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the first
scientific projects

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:31.850 align:middle line:84%
of any reasonable scale that
had from the very start,

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:37.640 align:middle line:84%
from the very first proposed
funding for it in 1990,

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:43.500 align:middle line:84%
a component of 3% set
aside for ethical, legal,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.450 align:middle line:90%
and social issue or LC.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.520 align:middle line:84%
And some of the conflicts
that are covered

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.440 align:middle line:84%
by the grantees in this
part of the Genome Project

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.180 align:middle line:84%
were genetic non-discrimination,
privacy, reproductive rights,

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.480 align:middle line:84%
cloning, psychological
stigmatization

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.910 align:middle line:84%
that can come from maybe
too much knowledge,

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.580 align:middle line:84%
clinical quality
control, what can

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.009 align:middle line:84%
happen with false positives
and false negatives

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.975 align:middle line:84%
in clinical exams, safety
and environmental issues,

00:02:12.975 --> 00:02:15.350 align:middle line:84%
such as the ones we were just
talking about and some more

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:18.320 align:middle line:90%
that I'll raise.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.030 align:middle line:84%
Uncertainties, not just
in quality control,

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.725 align:middle line:84%
but in the uncertainties
in testing minors.

00:02:24.725 --> 00:02:27.440 align:middle line:90%


00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.760 align:middle line:84%
Issues of diversity, both
biodiversity and human

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.420 align:middle line:84%
diversity, and commercialization
of the products.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:41.540 align:middle line:84%
Who owns my cells when I give
them to a hospital or a company

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:42.710 align:middle line:90%
to help cure me?

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:47.120 align:middle line:84%
Do they then own all
the patent rights?

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.850 align:middle line:84%
So the underlined
topics are the ones,

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.680 align:middle line:84%
the four underlined
topics we'll talk about

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.930 align:middle line:90%
in the next few slides.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.750 align:middle line:84%
In terms of non-discrimination,
this can go any direction.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:59.498 align:middle line:84%
It will go the direction
the market forces push it,

00:02:59.498 --> 00:03:00.290 align:middle line:90%
meaning the voters.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.960 align:middle line:90%


00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.710 align:middle line:84%
If we want to pass
really laws that

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:10.280 align:middle line:84%
say that if George Church
sequences his genome

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.860 align:middle line:84%
and he has to report that
sequence to his insurance

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.140 align:middle line:84%
company or worse yet the
insurance company is going

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.330 align:middle line:84%
and get it before
he wants it or not.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.240 align:middle line:84%
But on the other end
of the spectrum, and I

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.720 align:middle line:84%
hope the trend which will make
it much easier for us to share

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.640 align:middle line:90%
our data, or use our data--

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.260 align:middle line:84%
that's it-- is that the
Non-Discrimination in Health

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:35.570 align:middle line:84%
Insurance and Employment Act
was [? docked ?] in 1999,

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:43.670 align:middle line:84%
was introduced and passed,
which would extend employment

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:46.760 align:middle line:84%
protections in the
government sector

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.830 align:middle line:84%
to the private
sector to the extent

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.740 align:middle line:90%
that that can be generalized.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.360 align:middle line:84%
I don't know
exactly where that's

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.550 align:middle line:90%
going but one can hope that--

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:01.970 align:middle line:84%
I mean clearly we will always
do some kind of discrimination

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:08.780 align:middle line:84%
based on genetics
which can be assessed

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:15.428 align:middle line:84%
such as in an interview, you
know, height, friendliness,

00:04:15.428 --> 00:04:16.220 align:middle line:90%
that sort of thing.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.380 align:middle line:84%
But we won't
necessarily be doing it

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.140 align:middle line:90%
based on genetic sequence.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:24.080 align:middle line:90%
And these are tough issues.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.420 align:middle line:84%
It's the probabilistic
nature of the decisions

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.220 align:middle line:84%
that you make
during an interview

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.080 align:middle line:84%
different from the
probabilistic decision

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.380 align:middle line:84%
you would make
based on a DNA scan?

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:34.692 align:middle line:90%
Which is more accurate?

00:04:34.692 --> 00:04:36.150 align:middle line:84%
Do you want it to
be more accurate?

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.830 align:middle line:84%
Do you want it to
be less accurate?

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:38.640 align:middle line:90%
So on and so forth.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.560 align:middle line:84%
But clearly there's the
trend in terms of legislation

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:49.370 align:middle line:84%
is towards less information
being used for discrimination.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:51.380 align:middle line:84%
Certainly in the
insurance level.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:54.140 align:middle line:84%
And it's appropriate
in insurance

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:59.970 align:middle line:84%
because in a way that's supposed
to be a process by which risk

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:00.470 align:middle line:90%
is shared.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:03.620 align:middle line:90%


00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:05.810 align:middle line:90%
So the issue of races.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:12.440 align:middle line:84%
Typically races,
some people feel

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.720 align:middle line:84%
this is a scientifically
rigorous definition,

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:15.800 align:middle line:90%
others do not.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:22.610 align:middle line:84%
Some feel that it involves
very, very broad strokes

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.280 align:middle line:90%
in other organisms at least.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:30.510 align:middle line:84%
It can cover fairly detailed
bottlenecks in populations.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:33.080 align:middle line:84%
But in any case,
there are elements

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.830 align:middle line:84%
of population structure which
certainly are important,

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:39.170 align:middle line:84%
whether you ascribe
it to the major races

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:44.540 align:middle line:84%
or to minor,
smaller populations.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:46.490 align:middle line:84%
And examples here,
we've already talked

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.895 align:middle line:84%
about hemoglobin variants
evolved to resist malaria.

00:05:49.895 --> 00:05:51.950 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be one
of the themes today.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:56.390 align:middle line:90%


00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.738 align:middle line:84%
And as with the differences
in skin pigmentation,

00:05:58.738 --> 00:06:01.280 align:middle line:84%
the pressure of the environment
to develop a group-wide trait

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.210 align:middle line:84%
was powerful and can involve
a very small number of genes.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:14.330 align:middle line:84%
You get founder effects
where a particular population

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.970 align:middle line:84%
is highly enriched for
a particular disease

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:22.610 align:middle line:84%
such as Huntington's in the case
of Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:29.000 align:middle line:84%
or Tay-Sachs in some
Jewish populations.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.990 align:middle line:84%
So when you have these
well isolated populations,

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.690 align:middle line:84%
I think, you can see in the
population genetic literature

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:38.260 align:middle line:84%
how they can be used
in various ways.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:42.070 align:middle line:84%
But, overall, and it's
sort of the broadest level

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:47.560 align:middle line:84%
we all share a large
set of commonly acquired

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:48.970 align:middle line:90%
polymorphisms.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:53.260 align:middle line:84%
And even with ants, the
smallest subpopulation,

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.720 align:middle line:84%
it still behooves us
to look at the details

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:58.630 align:middle line:84%
of the individual
variations in DNA sequence

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:02.290 align:middle line:90%
in the haplotypes or genotypes.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:04.630 align:middle line:84%
I have two dangerous
slides in a row.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:08.140 align:middle line:84%
I could probably come up with
quite a few more, so could you.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:09.610 align:middle line:90%
But one of them is--

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:13.780 align:middle line:84%
this is really a very strong
advocate for her modeling.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.950 align:middle line:84%
And it's totally
anecdotal slide itself

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.970 align:middle line:84%
is that is non-scientific,
historical.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:25.430 align:middle line:84%
But this is one critics'
view of the huge influence

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:29.360 align:middle line:84%
that Lysenko had on genetics
and biology in the Soviet

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.130 align:middle line:84%
Union from the early
days of the revolution.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:42.380 align:middle line:84%
And his real pet theory
was the inheritance

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:43.760 align:middle line:90%
of acquired characteristics.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:45.135 align:middle line:84%
And there's nothing
intrinsically

00:07:45.135 --> 00:07:47.090 align:middle line:84%
wrong with in broad
strokes the inheritance

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.300 align:middle line:90%
of acquired characteristics.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.840 align:middle line:84%
It's not certainly not
common biological phenomenon.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:00.860 align:middle line:84%
And at the time, it was not
helping their biology progress

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.980 align:middle line:84%
at the same rate as the rest
of the world which was mainly

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:08.960 align:middle line:84%
pursuing the Mendelian
inheritance models.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.530 align:middle line:84%
And this critic in quotes
felt that his habit

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:15.050 align:middle line:90%
was to report only successes.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.150 align:middle line:84%
This is a really
feel good habit.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:21.410 align:middle line:84%
And his results were based on
extremely small sample sizes,

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:25.310 align:middle line:84%
inaccurate records, and the
almost total absence of control

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.560 align:middle line:90%
groups.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.110 align:middle line:84%
He made an early mistake
in the calculation which

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:33.690 align:middle line:84%
caused comments among other
specialists in his field

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.710 align:middle line:84%
and made him extremely negative
towards the use of mathematics

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.510 align:middle line:90%
and science.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.000 align:middle line:84%
Making mistakes
should not cause you

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.320 align:middle line:84%
to drop the use of
mathematics and science.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.453 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully, those
of you, probably

00:08:48.453 --> 00:08:50.870 align:middle line:84%
everybody in this class has
made a mistake in mathematics.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.990 align:middle line:84%
And, hopefully, none of
you will drop mathematics

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:56.870 align:middle line:90%
in your future biology.

00:08:56.870 --> 00:09:03.680 align:middle line:84%
And the second danger is the
danger of ethics-free science.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.880 align:middle line:84%
Now you get to the
truly remarkable story

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:14.840 align:middle line:84%
that in 1979, there was a
release of anthrax 836 spores

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:21.140 align:middle line:84%
and part of the
former Soviet Union.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:26.900 align:middle line:84%
And a few years later,
actually 1999, this book

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.580 align:middle line:84%
was published describing
what was behind that.

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:37.580 align:middle line:84%
And what was behind that
was decades earlier in 1953.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:41.870 align:middle line:84%
There was a leak
in one, I guess,

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:47.830 align:middle line:90%
of their anthrax developments.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:52.180 align:middle line:84%
And then 1956, they found
that one of the rodents

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:55.930 align:middle line:84%
that they captured in their
routine surveys of the sewers

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:58.720 align:middle line:84%
searching down a
possible anthrax,

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:01.060 align:middle line:84%
that actually a
strain had become

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:02.630 align:middle line:84%
much more virulent
than the original

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.420 align:middle line:90%
that they were working with.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:09.160 align:middle line:84%
And usually the response
of public health official

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:12.190 align:middle line:84%
or even a reasonable person
would be to kill this thing.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:17.170 align:middle line:84%
But instead they decided
that this was great.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:18.160 align:middle line:90%
Let's cultivate it.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:25.330 align:middle line:84%
And the idea was to install it
into these rockets that were

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.910 align:middle line:90%
targeted on Western cities.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:32.140 align:middle line:84%
And then that led eventually
to them depositing spores

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:35.770 align:middle line:84%
on their own people
accidentally of this greatly

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:37.480 align:middle line:90%
enhanced strain.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:42.790 align:middle line:84%
So the question to our community
as this genome engineering

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:45.940 align:middle line:84%
tools get easier and
easier where you basically

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:49.480 align:middle line:84%
can sequence genomes
inexpensively and even

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:52.210 align:middle line:84%
synthesize genomes
inexpensively.

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:55.720 align:middle line:84%
And much of this is going to
be in the public domain, what

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:58.360 align:middle line:84%
is to stop this from
being a very easily

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:07.750 align:middle line:84%
disguised and potentially very
inexpensive form of terrorism?

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:10.510 align:middle line:84%
And part of that
is that we do what

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:14.680 align:middle line:84%
we can do to improve
either detection tools

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:19.960 align:middle line:84%
or genome engineering tools
to make them more of a defense

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:22.330 align:middle line:90%
type than offense.

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:28.570 align:middle line:84%
But this was done in the
early days of recombinant DNA

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:30.700 align:middle line:84%
where the vectors
were designed such

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:34.600 align:middle line:84%
that if the vector ever
were to escape from the lab,

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:35.920 align:middle line:90%
it would die immediately.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.190 align:middle line:84%
It would be lacking the
nutrients it needed to grow.

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:41.890 align:middle line:84%
It would be very sensitive to
detergents that occur typically

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:44.360 align:middle line:90%
in sewers and so forth.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:47.170 align:middle line:84%
Now the vectors we use today,
those were not very robust.

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:48.820 align:middle line:84%
They didn't grow
very well in the lab

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:51.730 align:middle line:90%
as much less in the sewers.

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:56.710 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out that random
release is fairly low risk

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:57.310 align:middle line:90%
problem.

00:11:57.310 --> 00:12:00.130 align:middle line:84%
That was what we were
worried about in the 1970s

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:01.270 align:middle line:90%
with recombinant DNA.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:06.370 align:middle line:84%
But purposeful release of
genetically modified organisms

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:08.350 align:middle line:90%
is more of the issue today.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:11.200 align:middle line:84%
And, of course, genetically
modified organisms

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:14.890 align:middle line:84%
on this slide have been
created and engineered

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:20.290 align:middle line:84%
over the millennium,
maybe 10 millennium or so,

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:23.980 align:middle line:90%
without a license.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:25.880 align:middle line:84%
And they've been
very successful.

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:27.505 align:middle line:84%
You picture this
little weed-like thing

00:12:27.505 --> 00:12:32.650 align:middle line:84%
at the top left, and you end
up with this great 4th of July

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:34.150 align:middle line:90%
corn on the cob.

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:36.220 align:middle line:90%
And it's a hybrid corn.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:43.660 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, these dogs
range over three logarithms

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:49.660 align:middle line:84%
in adult mass would barely be
recognized as the same species

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:53.050 align:middle line:84%
if we didn't know them
and love them very well.

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:56.140 align:middle line:84%
But these are examples
of genetic engineering

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:59.050 align:middle line:90%
that was done pre-genomics.

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:03.550 align:middle line:84%
But now when we use
recombinant DNA in particular,

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:09.010 align:middle line:84%
but genetic any kind of
interspecific genetic

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:15.350 align:middle line:84%
modification, this definitely
raises environmental issues,

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:16.840 align:middle line:90%
especially in the developing--

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:23.860 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, in the say European
nations where they are wealthy,

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:25.660 align:middle line:84%
and they don't need
much improvement

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.186 align:middle line:90%
in their agricultural needs.

00:13:28.186 --> 00:13:32.080 align:middle line:90%


00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:34.540 align:middle line:84%
Some of the developing world
has very definite needs

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:37.180 align:middle line:84%
for genetics, or feel they
do, for genetically modified

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:38.030 align:middle line:90%
organisms.

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:41.980 align:middle line:84%
Some of these include
producing vaccines in plants.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.380 align:middle line:84%
Vaccines are one of the
most cost effective ways

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:49.930 align:middle line:84%
of generating public
health results

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:54.970 align:middle line:84%
but possibly even more effective
would be to have your bananas

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:56.735 align:middle line:84%
and other crops
contain vaccines.

00:13:56.735 --> 00:13:58.360 align:middle line:84%
And these have actually
been developed.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.810 align:middle line:84%
But it's been hard
getting them actually

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:06.550 align:middle line:84%
supplied due to concerns about
release of genetically modified

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:07.390 align:middle line:90%
organisms.

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:11.050 align:middle line:84%
Possible allergic
response and so forth.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:15.330 align:middle line:84%
Salt and drought tolerance
is extremely important.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:17.810 align:middle line:90%
They often come together.

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:21.820 align:middle line:84%
And there are a huge number
of drought and salt tolerant

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:24.550 align:middle line:84%
plants, so-called
resurrection plants.

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:28.390 align:middle line:84%
About 100 different,
completely different species

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:30.670 align:middle line:84%
which could provide
new genes that

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.400 align:middle line:84%
can be introduced into
non-drought tolerant plants

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:36.550 align:middle line:84%
and are being introduced
by scientists in developing

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:40.660 align:middle line:90%
worlds, such as Africa.

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:43.750 align:middle line:84%
Terminators were
originally, it's

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:46.720 align:middle line:84%
like this popular,
unpopular, popular again.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:49.270 align:middle line:84%
They were popular
with the companies

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:54.340 align:middle line:84%
that developed them for
reasons that we may not know.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:15:02.170 align:middle line:84%
But there was the
concern or the backlash

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:05.560 align:middle line:84%
was the companies are
doing this to preventing

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:07.090 align:middle line:90%
the farmers from reseeding.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:12.310 align:middle line:84%
By the terminator meant that
the next generation seeds

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:13.180 align:middle line:90%
would be useless.

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:17.980 align:middle line:84%
And it had been the habit
of a farmer to reseed.

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:20.590 align:middle line:84%
And so the terminators
didn't sell for a while.

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:23.560 align:middle line:84%
But then because of the
worry about dispersal

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:25.550 align:middle line:84%
of genetically
modified organisms,

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:29.970 align:middle line:84%
now terminators are becoming
more interesting again

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:31.570 align:middle line:90%
to a broad set of people.

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:35.020 align:middle line:84%
You talk about organic
farming, there is controversy

00:15:35.020 --> 00:15:37.810 align:middle line:84%
as to whether the
tightest definition

00:15:37.810 --> 00:15:40.840 align:middle line:84%
of this, which involves
no inorganic fertilizers

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:48.440 align:middle line:84%
like nitrates and
phosphates and so forth.

00:15:48.440 --> 00:15:50.952 align:middle line:84%
But that means a very
high animal load.

00:15:50.952 --> 00:15:52.660 align:middle line:84%
High animal load means
you need to use up

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:54.460 align:middle line:84%
a lot of your vegetable
crops in order

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:59.140 align:middle line:84%
to produce the fertilizer
for the other vegetables.

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:01.540 align:middle line:84%
And as you pointed out
that natural is not

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.360 align:middle line:90%
necessarily harmless.

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:07.360 align:middle line:84%
A wide variety of
naturally occurring

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:10.930 align:middle line:84%
compounds, natural pesticides,
are also carcinogens.

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.885 align:middle line:84%
And here's the laundry list
at the bottom of slide 43.

00:16:13.885 --> 00:16:19.870 align:middle line:90%


00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:21.010 align:middle line:90%
Cloning of stem cells.

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:23.650 align:middle line:90%


00:16:23.650 --> 00:16:30.190 align:middle line:84%
We have problems with
cloning definitely.

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:32.950 align:middle line:84%
In almost every species,
even the successful ones,

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:35.050 align:middle line:84%
there are a variety
of species which have

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:36.580 align:middle line:90%
been tried and not succeeded.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:38.380 align:middle line:84%
There's some that
have been tried.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:40.210 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at
the studies in detail

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:42.910 align:middle line:84%
they're examples of
developmental defects

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:47.920 align:middle line:84%
higher than expected
in that species ranging

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:50.835 align:middle line:90%
from a few percent on up.

00:16:50.835 --> 00:16:52.210 align:middle line:84%
And, obviously,
there's some kind

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:54.610 align:middle line:84%
of epigenetic
reprogramming that's

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.070 align:middle line:84%
occurring here where
you're not getting

00:16:57.070 --> 00:17:00.310 align:middle line:84%
all the right contributions
from the maternal and paternal

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:01.390 align:middle line:90%
genomes.

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:05.980 align:middle line:84%
And this can be possibly studied
with expression profiles.

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:10.900 align:middle line:84%
We can start employing
all of our automation

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:18.609 align:middle line:84%
to analyze how we can increase
the fraction of stem cells

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:28.700 align:middle line:84%
that we could take adult
cells and send them

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:36.680 align:middle line:84%
into different
lineages, or we can

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:40.430 align:middle line:84%
formulate ways to either
transform one adult stem

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:42.140 align:middle line:84%
cell into another or
an adult stem cell

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:46.190 align:middle line:84%
into a slightly more
primitive stem cell

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:50.810 align:middle line:84%
and still retain the advantages
of so-called therapeutic

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:58.370 align:middle line:84%
cloning, which
would be to maintain

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:00.635 align:middle line:84%
good histocompatibility,
say, with the patients.

00:18:00.635 --> 00:18:03.240 align:middle line:90%


00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.750 align:middle line:90%
So finally, education.

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:07.820 align:middle line:84%
Why should we bring up
education in a course?

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:11.540 align:middle line:90%


00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:13.700 align:middle line:84%
We've talked about
models of decision

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:15.260 align:middle line:90%
making in public health.

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:19.220 align:middle line:90%


00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:24.830 align:middle line:84%
But there's also a
similar set for education.

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:27.890 align:middle line:84%
We want to be able to deal
with uncertainty, complexity,

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:28.670 align:middle line:90%
quantification.

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:32.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure that I have introduced,
or we, have introduced plenty

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:35.677 align:middle line:84%
of uncertainty and complexity
in quantitation in your lives

00:18:35.677 --> 00:18:36.385 align:middle line:90%
with this course.

00:18:36.385 --> 00:18:38.540 align:middle line:84%
I apologize for the
parts that are painful.

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:45.200 align:middle line:84%
I hope that no pain, no gain
has some applicability here.

00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:47.750 align:middle line:84%
We want to-- a theme
in this course has been

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:50.210 align:middle line:90%
to cherish your exceptions.

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:54.130 align:middle line:84%
Collect them, and these
can be discoveries.

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:55.880 align:middle line:84%
They are at least going
to keep you honest

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:59.465 align:middle line:84%
and keep you from
making big mistakes.

00:18:59.465 --> 00:19:03.710 align:middle line:90%


00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:07.640 align:middle line:84%
We want to be able to
translate from one data type

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:10.430 align:middle line:84%
to another, from one conceptual
foundation to another,

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:14.570 align:middle line:84%
to integrate different either
adjacent conceptual spheres

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:15.680 align:middle line:90%
or very distant ones.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:18.300 align:middle line:90%


00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:23.730 align:middle line:84%
The way we do this,
slide 47, we need

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.460 align:middle line:84%
to have measures of
our progress, measures

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:30.150 align:middle line:84%
of the quality of the
underlying data and the models.

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:34.290 align:middle line:84%
And we've already done
this to some extent,

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:37.620 align:middle line:84%
basically, for three dimensional
structures and sequence

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:43.350 align:middle line:84%
data, the primary and
tertiary information.

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:46.668 align:middle line:84%
For X-ray diffraction-- this
dates back into the '60s--

00:19:46.668 --> 00:19:48.210 align:middle line:84%
we have measures of
data quality that

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:51.670 align:middle line:84%
include resolution, model
quality, which is the R

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:53.310 align:middle line:90%
factor we talked about before.

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:55.770 align:middle line:84%
We have ways of doing
similarity searches.

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:59.310 align:middle line:84%
For sequencing, a
more recent enterprise

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:02.250 align:middle line:84%
in the '80s, late
'80s, early '90s,

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:04.590 align:middle line:84%
we got the Genome
Project launched

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:08.070 align:middle line:84%
with thoughts of measuring
data quality in terms

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:09.840 align:middle line:90%
of discrepancy per base pair.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:15.120 align:middle line:84%
That should be less than 1
part in 10 to the fourth.

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:18.840 align:middle line:84%
The models, typically, are
models of protein conservation.

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:22.080 align:middle line:84%
And a similarity search is
one of the greatest killer

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:26.580 align:middle line:84%
applications of all times,
which is [? last. ?]

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:31.890 align:middle line:84%
And then for function
we're less far along.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:35.410 align:middle line:84%
We don't really have
accession numbers, as I said.

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:43.320 align:middle line:84%
We don't really have great ways
of doing similarity searches

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:49.020 align:middle line:84%
through say image databases or
similarity searches other than

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:51.168 align:middle line:90%
say correlation coefficients.

00:20:51.168 --> 00:20:52.710 align:middle line:84%
But I think this is
rapidly changing.

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:55.440 align:middle line:90%


00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:58.980 align:middle line:84%
When we start
applying these models,

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:04.140 align:middle line:84%
this is not only in
the education sense,

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:06.300 align:middle line:84%
but in the probing
these networks

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:08.100 align:middle line:84%
that we've been talking
about, even further

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:12.870 align:middle line:84%
beyond consideration
of neural networks

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:16.470 align:middle line:84%
and our interaction
with other organisms,

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:19.560 align:middle line:84%
kind of combining those
two, the neural networks

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:22.200 align:middle line:84%
and our interaction with
other organisms, ecology,

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.120 align:middle line:90%
is this notion of biophilia.

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:28.140 align:middle line:84%
Which is that we, as human
beings and other fairly

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:32.910 align:middle line:84%
intelligent animals, are
connected subconsciously

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:35.080 align:middle line:90%
to other living beings.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:37.740 align:middle line:84%
It is clear that no matter
how urban or what culture

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:39.180 align:middle line:84%
you're talking
about, snake dreams

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:40.860 align:middle line:90%
figure in very prominently.

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:44.340 align:middle line:84%
And this has to do with
the need of primates

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:46.830 align:middle line:84%
to avoid and track snakes
from their vicinity

00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:49.800 align:middle line:90%
and the vicinity of the tribe.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:52.110 align:middle line:90%
Little animals are cute.

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:54.793 align:middle line:90%
We just know that.

00:21:54.793 --> 00:21:56.460 align:middle line:84%
And they are much
cuter than the adults,

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:58.835 align:middle line:84%
no matter what the animal is,
with the possible exception

00:21:58.835 --> 00:21:59.400 align:middle line:90%
of snakes.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.690 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:01.690 --> 00:22:07.950 align:middle line:84%
And there are
anecdotal, at least,

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:12.330 align:middle line:84%
possibly well-characterized
effects of the green fractals

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:15.930 align:middle line:84%
that we like to see
in trees and plants.

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:18.450 align:middle line:84%
And taking this one step
further from just this kind

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:22.380 align:middle line:84%
of very stimulating thought that
we actually have co-evolved,

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.450 align:middle line:84%
our nervous system
has co-evolved for so

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:25.680 align:middle line:90%
long with these other things.

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:28.920 align:middle line:84%
It would be natural for us
to be very attuned to them

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:32.130 align:middle line:84%
because our survival would
have depended upon our ability

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:38.490 align:middle line:84%
to avoid snakes and to deal
with green versus brown shrubs.

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:43.320 align:middle line:84%
And if you take that
one step further,

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:48.660 align:middle line:84%
much of what we do
in the humanities say

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:52.770 align:middle line:84%
is affected our aesthetics
and our approaches to it,

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:56.620 align:middle line:84%
our beliefs are affected
by this heritage.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:58.920 align:middle line:84%
And this is what E.O
Wilson has championed.

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:03.580 align:middle line:84%
And I would say it's quite
controversial as it should be.

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:06.090 align:middle line:84%
But in general, long separated
fields come together--

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:08.190 align:middle line:84%
it's this consilience
definition--

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:09.480 align:middle line:90%
and they create new insights.

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:13.650 align:middle line:84%
Like chemistry and genetics
brings us molecular biology.

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:15.960 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, is
all of human endeavor

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:18.590 align:middle line:84%
ready for such a thing,
from religious feelings

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:20.890 align:middle line:90%
to financial markets and so on?

00:23:20.890 --> 00:23:23.700 align:middle line:84%
And whether or not,
how might genomics

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:25.710 align:middle line:84%
and computational
biology contribute?

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:30.690 align:middle line:84%
It's surprising sometimes when
these things do contribute.

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:32.790 align:middle line:84%
And here's some
speculation on that.

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:36.810 align:middle line:84%
We have improving
imaging methods.

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:39.990 align:middle line:84%
We talked about imaging in
the context of in situs.

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:42.840 align:middle line:84%
Some very functional
versions of this

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:46.750 align:middle line:84%
are positron emission tomography
and magnetic resonance imaging,

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:49.950 align:middle line:84%
which allow you to monitor
such things as blood

00:23:49.950 --> 00:24:00.060 align:middle line:84%
flow or metabolic uptake in
different parts of an actively

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:01.990 align:middle line:90%
metabolizing brain.

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:08.142 align:middle line:84%
And here you can inject
some O-15 labeled compound.

00:24:08.142 --> 00:24:09.600 align:middle line:84%
And you can get an
image resolution

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:11.310 align:middle line:84%
on the order of
nine millimeters.

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:16.350 align:middle line:84%
OK so the voxels
here, volume elements,

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:20.250 align:middle line:90%
are limited in that range.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:23.940 align:middle line:84%
And sort of in the 20 seconds
is sort of the time frame

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:25.740 align:middle line:90%
that you're working in.

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:28.200 align:middle line:84%
And this has been applied
to a whole variety

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:32.250 align:middle line:90%
of interesting behavioral tasks.

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:36.120 align:middle line:84%
Almost anything you can
think of counting memory,

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:37.740 align:middle line:90%
so forth in humans.

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:41.430 align:middle line:84%
And you can map the parts of the
brain that are differentially

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:45.390 align:middle line:84%
responsive to a control and
an experimental time frame

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:48.180 align:middle line:84%
for the same patient
in the same apparatus.

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:49.770 align:middle line:84%
And here, this is
just to show you

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:51.937 align:middle line:84%
how far I can go and connect
to what we were talking

00:24:51.937 --> 00:24:54.480 align:middle line:84%
about in the
previous slide, here

00:24:54.480 --> 00:25:02.130 align:middle line:84%
religious subjects, patients,
have been the differential

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:05.010 align:middle line:84%
parts of the brain
monitored here

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:07.860 align:middle line:84%
are in a religious
recitation process

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:11.100 align:middle line:90%
and in arresting control.

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:14.140 align:middle line:84%
And these are all
the P less than P

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:22.380 align:middle line:84%
to the 0.001 significance by
the very complicated statistics

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:30.270 align:middle line:84%
that are used in the analysis
of these functional maps.

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:34.260 align:middle line:84%
Now not to leave us on
that note but to connect it

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:38.070 align:middle line:84%
more to genomics and
computational biology

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:41.830 align:middle line:84%
is more in the heart
of this course.

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:46.650 align:middle line:84%
Here's an example how magnetic
resonance imaging, which

00:25:46.650 --> 00:25:49.110 align:middle line:84%
can be applied to
gene expression, how

00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:51.210 align:middle line:84%
we bring these two
together, kind of imaging

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:52.510 align:middle line:90%
gene expression.

00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:57.260 align:middle line:84%
And one of the powers of this in
contrast the positron emission

00:25:57.260 --> 00:26:00.380 align:middle line:84%
tomography had about a
9 millimeter resolution.

00:26:00.380 --> 00:26:02.420 align:middle line:84%
This has a 10 micron
resolution, sort

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:04.820 align:middle line:84%
of in the optical
resolution range.

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:07.970 align:middle line:84%
But unlike optical
methods, this will work

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:11.420 align:middle line:90%
for impact opaque organisms.

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:15.380 align:middle line:84%
And, finally, so MRI
will do that in general.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:17.120 align:middle line:84%
But to connect it
to gene expression,

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:19.970 align:middle line:84%
you need to tag the gene
expression such way,

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:22.280 align:middle line:84%
and we have green
fluorescent protein,

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:25.610 align:middle line:84%
we have other colors
fluorescent protein.

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:28.503 align:middle line:84%
We have luciferase, data
glycosides, and so forth.

00:26:28.503 --> 00:26:30.170 align:middle line:84%
One of those has to
be turn on something

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:33.710 align:middle line:84%
that has sufficient contrast
and magnetic resonance imaging.

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:39.980 align:middle line:84%
And an example of that is a way
of caging a gadolinium ion such

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.920 align:middle line:84%
that you can see this
little galactic tyrannous

00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:46.310 align:middle line:84%
ring at the top
right is covering it.

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:49.760 align:middle line:84%
And the little red bond there is
cleaved by beta galactosidase,

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:51.200 align:middle line:90%
lacZ fusions.

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:53.495 align:middle line:84%
And so you can now make your
same reporter constructs

00:26:53.495 --> 00:26:55.370 align:middle line:84%
that have been used
for making, say,

00:26:55.370 --> 00:26:58.430 align:middle line:84%
blue cells with a
core metric indicator

00:26:58.430 --> 00:27:00.260 align:middle line:90%
with optical wavelengths.

00:27:00.260 --> 00:27:02.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, now with
magnetic resonance,

00:27:02.150 --> 00:27:04.145 align:middle line:84%
imaging released
that gadolinium.

00:27:04.145 --> 00:27:06.650 align:middle line:84%
So it's now accessible
to the solvent,

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:08.450 align:middle line:84%
has a different
resonance, and you

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:10.190 align:middle line:90%
can get sharp contrast here.

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:13.310 align:middle line:84%
In a living organism on the
top, with 10 micron resolution,

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:15.230 align:middle line:84%
as opposed to a
fixed organism, which

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:20.150 align:middle line:84%
is required for getting the
whole-mount in situ staining

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:21.650 align:middle line:90%
with lacZ.

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:25.610 align:middle line:84%
So I think, this is a very
interesting combination

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:30.020 align:middle line:84%
of being able to use something
alive in opaque tissue

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:34.280 align:middle line:90%
with a genomic tag of sorts.

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:38.160 align:middle line:90%


00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:43.630 align:middle line:84%
Well, on that religious
note, we will sort of

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:49.200 align:middle line:84%
wrap up the course, my part
of it, the lecture part.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:52.170 align:middle line:84%
And in so doing, I
want to especially give

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:55.860 align:middle line:84%
many, many thanks not just
to this year's TFs, which

00:27:55.860 --> 00:27:58.890 align:middle line:84%
we will get to in just a
moment, but the ones that helped

00:27:58.890 --> 00:28:00.540 align:middle line:90%
start this phase of the course.

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:02.880 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the course
goes back to '88.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:06.120 align:middle line:84%
But these are some
of the Teaching

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:11.970 align:middle line:90%
Fellows in 1999 and 2000, 2001.

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:16.050 align:middle line:84%
One of these Teaching Fellows,
[? Suzanne ?] [? Camille, ?]

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:19.745 align:middle line:84%
has stayed with us as a head
Teaching Fellow this year.

00:28:19.745 --> 00:28:21.750 align:middle line:90%
She was up to the top of--

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:25.260 align:middle line:84%
and I'm very thankful to
every one here, Woody,

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:30.300 align:middle line:84%
[? June, ?] [? Len, ?] Tom,
[? John, ?] [? Juang ?]

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:33.570 align:middle line:84%
[? Hong, ?] Gary, and
[? Lachmans. ?] If any of you

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:37.080 align:middle line:84%
are here, could you kind of
stand up, wave, just wave.

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:38.430 align:middle line:90%
Thank you very much.

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:40.890 align:middle line:90%
Really, I love it.

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:48.840 align:middle line:84%
And if this course is
to continue to survive,

00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:52.390 align:middle line:90%
we need TFs for next year.

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:55.710 align:middle line:84%
So if any of you feel that
you have the right stuff

00:28:55.710 --> 00:29:00.330 align:middle line:84%
and feel that this course should
survive, please contact us.

00:29:00.330 --> 00:29:04.260 align:middle line:90%


00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:09.780 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully, it'll be some
finite positive number.

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:12.420 align:middle line:84%
We really love the
projects, and we'd love--

00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:15.090 align:middle line:84%
and in the past,
many students have

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:18.360 align:middle line:84%
been reluctant to stop
working on the project

00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:21.190 align:middle line:84%
after the course is over and
their grade has been assigned

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:23.370 align:middle line:90%
and so forth.

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:27.360 align:middle line:84%
And that nothing could make me
happier than to have do that.

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:30.880 align:middle line:84%
There's a limit to just how
much I can help you do that.

00:29:30.880 --> 00:29:33.420 align:middle line:84%
But if anything
I can do to help,

00:29:33.420 --> 00:29:35.880 align:middle line:84%
providing additional
mentoring and so forth,

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:37.680 align:middle line:90%
I'd love to do that.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:42.940 align:middle line:84%
Some of these have even resulted
in publications later on.

00:29:42.940 --> 00:29:47.790 align:middle line:84%
We started this course, slide
four of the first lecture,

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:50.190 align:middle line:84%
actually the first real
slide or the first lecture,

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:52.575 align:middle line:84%
was on the origins
of zeros and ones.

00:29:52.575 --> 00:29:55.110 align:middle line:90%


00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:59.790 align:middle line:84%
Kind of a play on the
101 course number,

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:02.070 align:middle line:84%
where did the binary
code come from?

00:30:02.070 --> 00:30:05.190 align:middle line:84%
And you could
ascribe it to Leibniz

00:30:05.190 --> 00:30:13.170 align:middle line:84%
or one of the modern 18th
century mathematicians.

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:18.600 align:middle line:84%
But Leibniz himself
found that it had already

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:24.870 align:middle line:84%
been invented about
5,000 years ago or so

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:27.150 align:middle line:90%
by China's first emperor.

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:31.380 align:middle line:84%
And since then, some
people have gone so far as

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:39.300 align:middle line:84%
to take this binary coding, the
etching, which has 64 hexagons

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:41.370 align:middle line:84%
and has arranged
it in such a way

00:30:41.370 --> 00:30:44.670 align:middle line:84%
that it has decoded
it so that it actually

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:48.880 align:middle line:84%
fits in with the geniculate
code which has 64 codons.

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:51.750 align:middle line:84%
So for those of you who are
students of the etching,

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:56.200 align:middle line:84%
hopefully, it now has
new meaning for you.

00:30:56.200 --> 00:31:01.440 align:middle line:84%
And to emphasize this
yin and yang symbol here,

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:03.960 align:middle line:84%
remember that the
purpose of having

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:08.040 align:middle line:84%
the black dot in the white zone
and the reciprocal white dot

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:10.980 align:middle line:84%
in the black zone is to remind
you that things are not just

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:11.860 align:middle line:90%
black and white.

00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:14.400 align:middle line:90%
They're not just zeros and ones.

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:20.160 align:middle line:84%
They have more complexity, and
they are constantly in change.

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:24.450 align:middle line:84%
This is the book of changes,
and this course is about change.

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:27.540 align:middle line:84%
And you should-- you do
computational biology.

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:30.467 align:middle line:90%


00:31:30.467 --> 00:31:31.800 align:middle line:90%
It's not just about computation.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.410 align:middle line:84%
It's not by keeping yourself
busy at the computer,

00:31:34.410 --> 00:31:36.120 align:middle line:84%
keeping your computers
busy all the time.

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:40.630 align:middle line:84%
It's about thinking, thinking
as broadly as you can.

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:42.600 align:middle line:90%
So thank you very much.

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:45.950 align:middle line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:45.950 --> 00:31:47.000 align:middle line:90%